er to resemble a bright, sharply bounded object.\n\nSIMBAD\u2019s other entries show that astronomers describe NGC 2632 in more than one way. The database lists a parallax of 5.371 milliarcseconds, proper motion of \u221236.047 and \u221212.917 milliarcseconds per year, and a radial velocity of 34.94 kilometers per second. These measurements are not visual features to look for through binoculars. They are part of the cluster\u2019s catalog record, quantities used to describe position and motion.\n\nFor an observer, though, the angular size is the most immediate figure. It hints at the kind of view to seek: not a tiny jewel isolated against black, but a broad arrangement that benefits from space around it. Start with a wide view, then take time to notice how the stars occupy it. NGC 2632 rewards that patient approach\u2014not with a single dramatic point, but with the experience of a cluster spread across the field."
